# D46 rider (2026-09-04) — `Option[T]` gets INTRINSIC structural equality, but
# ONLY when its element is itself comparable. `Point` has no `Equatable`, so
# `Option[Point] == Option[Point]` is refused exactly as a tuple containing
# `Point` is, and the diagnostic blames `Point` — the type the derive goes on —
# never `Option`, which the author cannot annotate.
#
# `Option` and `Result` are ENUMS, so D46 half 2's literal text would have
# rejected them forever, including `Some(1) == Some(1)`. The rider settles that
# collision on the ANNOTATABILITY rationale: declarable ⇒ require `@derive`;
# non-declarable ⇒ intrinsic when the elements are comparable.

struct Point:
    int x
    int y

void main():
    Option[Point] a = Some(Point(1, 2))
    Option[Point] b = Some(Point(1, 2))
    print(a == b)
